1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
When a team shares a Cloud application,

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,000
you hit a common problem.

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Everyone's setup drifts and the app

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
behaves a little differently on each machine,

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
until nobody can reproduce a bug.

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The fix is to put your config under

7
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
version control right next to the code.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This lesson covers what to commit and why.

9
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's break it down. Let's draw

10
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
the line between what you commit and what you don't,

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
because getting it wrong breaks teammates.

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,000
On the committed side,

13
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
your project Cloud.md and

14
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
your project settings file belong in Git.

15
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Everyone who clones the repo then gets

16
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
the same instructions and the same permissions by default,

17
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
which is exactly what you want.

18
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
On the other side sits your personal machine local config.

19
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Your local settings file and a personal Cloud.local.md,

20
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,000
stay out of the repo,

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
get ignored on purpose, never team shared.

22
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Those hold individual tweaks,

23
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
and if you committed them,

24
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
you'd override a teammate's own setup.

25
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
The rule is simple,

26
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:02,000
shared behavior gets committed.

27
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Personal preferences stay local.

28
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Get that split right and nobody steps on anybody.

29
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
You might wonder why this is worth the discipline.

30
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Committing config isn't bureaucracy,

31
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
it's how a whole team ends up with

32
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
one consistent Cloud instead of many slightly different ones.

33
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
When the config is in the repo,

34
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
everyone pulls the same instructions and the same permissions,

35
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
so nobody's running a secret variation.

36
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
A brand new teammate clones

37
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
the project and is productive right away,

38
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,000
because the behavior comes with

39
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
the code instead of living in someone's head.

40
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Best of all, the app's behavior is reproducible.

41
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It's not a matter of machine-by-machine luck

42
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
or whatever somebody happened to configure locally.

43
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Shared config means the application acts the same on every desk,

44
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
which is the entire point of putting it under control.

45
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Concretely, what goes into the repository?

46
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Three things you'll check in every time.

47
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
First, your Cloud.md,

48
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
the project instructions that shape how Cloud works in this code base,

49
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
shared with the whole team so everyone reads from the same page.

50
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
Second, your project settings file,

51
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,000
which holds the permissions,

52
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
the hooks, and the model choice that everyone should run with.

53
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Third, your prompts and your schemas,

54
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
the system prompts and tool schemas your app actually depends on,

55
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
versioned right beside the code they work with.

56
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Cloud.md, settings, prompts, and schemas.

57
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
If any one of those lives only on your laptop,

58
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
a teammate is running a different application than you are,

59
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
and you won't find out until something breaks.

60
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,000
One config value deserves a closer look,

61
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
and that's the model ID your app calls.

62
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Here's the key idea.

63
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Each model ID is a fixed snapshot.

64
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
It's not a moving target that quietly upgrades under you.

65
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
The weights behind that exact ID stay the same over its lifetime.

66
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
So when you pin that ID in your committed config,

67
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
you're guaranteeing every run and

68
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
every teammate hits the same model with the same behavior.

69
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
When a newer model comes along,

70
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
it ships under a brand new ID,

71
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
never by silently swapping what your old ID points to.

72
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
That's why you commit the exact identifier

73
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
rather than some vague latest pointer.

74
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Reproducible behavior starts with knowing

75
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
precisely which model answered,

76
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
and version control is where you record it.

77
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,000
When your prompts and config evolve,

78
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
you'll want to version them,

79
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
and semantic versioning gives you a shared language for that.

80
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
It's three numbers, and each one means something specific.

81
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
The first number is the major version.

82
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
You bump it for a breaking change,

83
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
one that isn't backward compatible

84
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
with what people were relying on before.

85
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
The middle number is the minor version.

86
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
You bump it when you add new functionality

87
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
that still works for existing callers.

88
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
The last number is the patch.

89
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
You bump that for a small, backward compatible fix

90
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
that isn't supposed to change behavior at all.

91
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Major, minor, patch.

92
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
The trap to avoid is bumping patch for a breaking change,

93
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
because that quietly tells everyone

94
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
it's safe to upgrade when it isn't.

95
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Let's zoom out and see how all of this

96
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
rides the normal delivery stages.

97
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Version control plugs your Claude work

98
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
straight into the same lifecycle as any other software.

99
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
You develop a change on a branch,

100
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and that includes the config and the prompts,

101
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
not just the code.

102
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
You review the diff, which is where behavior shifts

103
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
get caught before they reach a user.

104
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Then you test and gate the change,

105
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
and once it passes, you deploy the merged result.

106
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
The thing to notice is that the model-facing work

107
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
isn't off in its own special process.

108
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
It rides the same pipeline as everything else.

109
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
So a prompt change moves from idea to production

110
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
with the same checks a code change gets.

111
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Let me contrast two ways the same edit can happen,

112
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
because the difference is night and day.

113
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
A tracked change is a prompt or config edit

114
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
that lives in a comment.

115
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
There's a diff showing what changed,

116
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
an author, a reason in the message,

117
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
and a clean way to roll it back if it misbehaves.

118
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Now, the untracked version.

119
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
That same edit happens in a chat window

120
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
or a personal file that never gets committed.

121
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Nobody else can see it, nobody can review it,

122
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and nobody can reproduce it later.

123
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
That's exactly how drift creeps into a project,

124
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
one invisible tweak at a time.

125
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
The whole goal of this lesson

126
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
is to keep every behavior-shaping change

127
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,000
on the tracked side of that line.

128
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
One more layer makes this really solid,

129
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
and that's continuous integration, or CI.

130
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
These are automated checks that run before a change merges,

131
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
and three of them matter for config.

132
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
The first validates your config.

133
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
A CI step parses your settings and your clod, .md,

134
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
so a broken file gets caught right there

135
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
instead of in production.

136
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
The second checks your schemas.

137
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The pipeline validates your tool and output schemas

138
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:06,000
against their spec,

139
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
so a malformed schema fails the build early.

140
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
The third gates your prompts.

141
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Automated checks can diff and even lint prompt changes,

142
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
flagging risky edits for a human

143
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
before the merge goes through.

144
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Validate config, check schemas, gate prompts.

145
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
That's your safety net running on every single change.

146
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Here's the bottom line.

147
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Committing config gives your team one reproducible clod.

148
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Project clod.md, settings, prompts,

149
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
and pinned model IDs all go into Git.

150
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Personal files stay out.

151
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
One thing to watch, an untracked edit is invisible drift.

152
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
That wraps up committing config into the lifecycle.

153
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Catch you in the next one.

